{"text": "640802501\n\nQuestions for SAMHSA\n\n1. Inalletter to Representative Bonilla, SAMHSA said that the biggest obstacle to\ncomplying with the SAMHSA rule is ensuring the completeness and accuracy of the States\u2019\nlists of outlets. | take this to mean that State under-enforcement of youth access laws,\nshoplifting, community ignorance, or lack of state governmental support are not particular\nproblems. Were you surprised by this finding?\n\n2, SAMHSA has stated that a review of studies shows that underage sales rates are\nbelow 15 percent when combined with penalties, community education efforts, and vendor\neducation. Have you found this same correlation between the combination of penalties and\neducation and low underage Sales rates in the 40 state reports that you have already\nreceived?\n\n3, Based on the information that you have received from the block grant information\nsubmitted by the States, what is the average rate of underage sales in the random\nunannounced inspections? What is the range of compliance? Do you see any trends of\nimproved compliance?\n\n4. Can you tell us how much the Federal Government has spent in overseeing the\nSynar Amendment program since its inception? What percentage of the total requested\nauthorization does administration of the Synar Amendment constitute? What percentage of\nthe total requested reauthorization represents the substance abuse, prevention, and\ntreatment block grant funds?\n\n5. _ Ina recent letter to Representative Borilla, SAMHSA seemed to attribute the delay in\npromulgating the final rule to concerns that the Synar Amendment was an unfunded\nmandate. Have any States expressed in their reports an inabilty to carry out the Synar\n\u2018Amendment because of lack of funding?\n\n6. Has SAMHSA found that the Synar Amendment offers it sufficient flexibility in\ndeveloping performance targets with the States?\n\n7. Despite the ambitious nature of the Synar Amendment's goals, SAMHSA has stated\nthat it expects every state to meet the compliance requirements within 5 years of state\nimplementation. Do you anticipate any events in the next years that would interfere with\nthis projection?\n\n8. Thirty-three States have enacted laws punishing youth purchase or possession of\ntobacco. Although SAMHSA rejected this approach in its final rule, it appears to be gaining\npopularity at the State level. Has SAMHSA had the opportunity to evaiuate the impact of\nthese laws?\n\n9. _Anevaluation of retailer training programs commissioned by NCI concluded that the\n\u2018We Card\u2019 program is \u201cexcellent in showing the array of methods, techniques and store\npolicies that merchants and their employees can use to prevent inadvertent underage\ntobacco sales...\u201d Has SAMHSA been in contact with NCI about this conclusion? Has\nSAMHSA considered incorporating the We Card program into a list of recommancen!\nresource materials? 640802501\n\nPRODUCED FROM B&W WEB SITE",
